it's still plenty to digest in the  aftermath of a historic day for the  National Football League  a pair of overtime conference  championship games first time that's  ever happened in Peter King was in  Kansas City and after that epic victory  by Tom Brady the 13 play 75-yard Drive  in overtime with three third and tens  converted along the way Peter had the  opportunity to sit down with Tom Brady  and have a conversation what were your  big takeaways from your time with Brady  in the locker room after the game Mike I  can just say this I sat with him for  seven minutes on these little wooden  stools in the visiting locker room and  there was mayhem around us pretty much  it was a jam packed shoulder-to-shoulder  locker room but I sat on the stool right  next to him and what was so interesting  was that I'd say he was as I as I wrote  he was kind of equal parts incredulous  and grateful when when I spoke to him  because I mean at one point he said to  me and I'll quote you know this is hard  for me to imagine nine Super Bowls I  know it's ridiculous and so I mean he  understands that going to nine Super  Bowls in 18 years is absolutely it's  probably something that's never going to  happen again it certainly is something  that he never ever could imagine being  the 199th player picked in 2000 but as  far as this game goes the most  interesting part of this game is what he  told me about the game planning for this  game he told me that they walked into  the players the offensive players walked  into the meeting at 11 o'clock that  morning six and a half hours before the  game at the Western Crown Center hotel a  ballroom there and Josh McDaniels had  eight new plays to add to the game plan  that these guys had to get up and walk  through they ended up running four of  those plays during the game  all four positive yards and so Brady's  point was he said Peter  don't forget continuity when you talk  about what we have done here you know  Ivan fears the running backs coach he's  been there 19 years Dantes carnet with  with every year with with Bill Belichick  Dantes Carnegie a 17 of 19 on the  offensive line Josh McDaniels 15 of 19  quarterback coach and offensive  coordinator that's what allows them to  speak this language when the quarterback  in all the influential coaches have been  together they can change on the fly and  do things that appear to be impossible  to do because they are able to speak the  same language for such a long period of  time yeah I mean that's a great point  and the more comfort there is the more  continuity as you say the easier it is  to figure out these problems figure out  what you need to do spot the tendency  spot the towels in your own game in the  in the opposing teams game and be ready  to go when when it's time to get it done  Brady himself did you get any impression  from him and I got the impression from  reading your column that he does view  this is one of his his best performances  ever six Super Bowl wins potentially  coming against the Rams and what a  bookend that would be he started his  career in his second season winning the  Super Bowl over the Rams 17 years later  here we are with the opportunity for  number six you get any impression any  hint whatsoever that if he would win  number six it would be  see you later forget about playing until  I'm 45 this is the right moment to walk  off the stage I don't think so Mike I  got asked that yesterday on Twitter and  I just can't see it and again I don't  know anything I don't really know Brady  that well I know him pretty well but I  don't he's never even hinted at that to  me and I've never heard him hint that to  anybody the fact is he loves Mike you  would say geez you know there's no  better way to walk off do a Jerome  Bettis do a John Elway but he just isn't  really wired like that  he likes every part of his job he's  gonna love going to see Tom house in the  spring in Los Angeles to work on  throwing mechanics he just absolutely  loves it he's told me five times over  the years that there's nothing that I  like doing better than playing football  why would I volunteer old me this a  couple of years ago  why would I voluntarily leave the game  if I'm still able to play so I don't see  him walking away there's something to be  said though for walking away before it  gets to the point where it's painfully  obvious that you're struggling and there  was a point this year Peter where a lot  of people almost gleefully suggesting  that father time had finally caught up  to him and whacked him over the head  with a giant hourglass and and the the I  think the popular opinion became that  there was an injury that that we didn't  know about and and I can't help but  wonder and and I I don't believe that  the rules require a player to disclose  all injuries to the team if you've got  this side gig with Alex Guerrero well  you can go get treatment on any injury  you have and the team never has a record  of it the team never treats you for it  you handle it all on your own then I  guess that's a legitimate way that's a  loophole when it comes to concealing  injuries but if he had a knee injury it  never showed up on any of the injury  reports and the thinking now is that  that's what went on and now he's healthy  and if he can stay healthy who knows how  long he can continue but at some point  at some point  father time will win and if that's after  he turns 45 and we never find out so be  it  but there's a chance between now and 45  it's gonna happen and the longer he  hangs around the the greater the chance  he's gonna have kind of an ugly sluggish  embarrassing end and and I think that in  hindsight he would say probably would  have been better to walk away when I was  on top I just don't see it and the  reason is that he'll know everybody  you'll know we'll all know and I agree  with you about the injuries you know  remember a couple of years ago when  there was the Giselle concussion thing I  mean Tom Brady is not going to  voluntarily ever say anything about an  injury unless it's  diagnosed by the team it's you know he's  got to get surgery or something like  that he just isn't gonna do that and you  know the team can say basically Tom  Brady parenthesis leg probable you know  and and so and again I don't think that  that is going to be the reason why he  would ever walk away I think he will  walk away when he feels like or when  when Bill Belichick goes and gets Tommy  touchdown from Cal Lutheran and and and  Brady then has an option is he gonna go  play one year for somebody or is he just  gonna retire so I mean I think it's more  likely going to be that Mike that he  will confront the end of his career and  he'll decide what to do  but I just I just think it's still a  couple of years away at least what's  your gut feeling if the Patriots decide  it's over before he decides it's over  would he go play that year or two Joe  Montana style in Kansas City with  another team I think he would and I  think that I think events of this year  may have something to do with it Mike  and I'll tell you what I mean  you know Daniel Jeremiah of NFL Network  put out his first mock draft this week  or last week and he had the Patriots at  whatever number 31 or 30 whatever it is  picking Kyler Murray and that really  struck a chord with me and I'll tell you  why remember last year I forget if it  was Jeremiah it might have been  Jeff Darlington I really forget who was  but on the draft coverage that night may  have been Ian Rapoport I just don't  recall on the draft coverage that night  somebody said that the New England  Patriots earlier that week had spent  time you know with Baker Mayfield and  we're interested in Baker Mayfield and  obviously it didn't matter he got picked  number one overall but that sort of told  me that okay Josh McDaniels here's your  job and whoever is it you know is going  to be the the guy who Belichick in his  department trusts the most tell 